insert into public.assessment_stimuli(code,title,content,stimulus_type) values('KLI-ITP-R1','Mangrove Restoration','Mangrove forests protect tropical coastlines by reducing wave energy and trapping sediment. For many years, restoration projects focused mainly on planting large numbers of seedlings. Results were inconsistent because planners sometimes chose unsuitable species or ignored tidal patterns. More recent projects begin by restoring the movement of water and examining why the original forest disappeared. In some locations, natural regeneration then occurs without extensive planting. Community participation is also important. Residents can identify past changes in fishing grounds and monitor young trees after storms. Restoration is not a rapid solution: a planted area may look successful during its first year yet fail later if water becomes too deep or too salty. Researchers therefore evaluate projects over several seasons and measure ecological functions, such as shoreline stability and nursery habitat for fish, rather than counting trees alone.','reading') on conflict(code) do update set title=excluded.title,content=excluded.content;
insert into public.assessment_stimuli(code,title,content,stimulus_type) values('KLI-ITP-R2','Sleep and Memory','Sleep is not simply a period during which the brain becomes inactive. Research indicates that several stages of sleep contribute differently to memory. After people learn a physical skill, certain patterns of brain activity reappear during sleep, suggesting that the brain is strengthening recently formed connections. Other stages appear especially important for integrating new facts with existing knowledge. This does not mean that sleeping immediately produces perfect recall. The quality of learning before sleep, emotional stress, and interruptions during the night also influence performance. Researchers warn that staying awake to study for additional hours can be counterproductive when it reduces both attention during learning and later consolidation. Short naps may provide limited benefits, but they do not consistently replace a full night of sleep. Consequently, effective study plans distribute practice across several days and protect regular sleep rather than treating rest as time lost.','reading') on conflict(code) do update set title=excluded.title,content=excluded.content;
insert into public.assessment_stimuli(code,title,content,stimulus_type) values('KLI-ITP-R3','Urban Bus Networks','Cities often attempt to improve public transportation by purchasing new buses, but vehicles alone do not guarantee better service. Reliability depends on how routes, schedules, and street space are managed. When buses share congested lanes with private cars, even a large fleet may move slowly and arrive in groups rather than at regular intervals. Dedicated lanes can reduce this problem, although their introduction may face opposition from drivers and nearby businesses. Transit agencies also use real-time location data to adjust service. If several buses on one route are delayed, controllers may shorten a trip or direct another vehicle to begin farther along the line. Such interventions can inconvenience some passengers, so agencies must explain them clearly. The most effective systems combine infrastructure, operational decisions, and accurate passenger information. Their success is measured not only by speed but also by whether riders can predict when they will arrive.','reading') on conflict(code) do update set title=excluded.title,content=excluded.content;
insert into public.assessment_stimuli(code,title,content,stimulus_type) values('KLI-ITP-R4','Bilingual Language Use','Bilingual speakers do not store two languages in completely separate mental compartments. Evidence from experiments suggests that words from both languages can become active even when only one language is being used. This simultaneous activation may occasionally slow the selection of a word, but it also gives speakers flexible ways to express ideas. The language chosen in a conversation depends on topic, setting, relationship, and social expectations. Switching languages is therefore not necessarily evidence of confusion; it can signal group identity, quote another person, or make a technical concept more precise. Nevertheless, bilingual ability is rarely equal across every domain. A person may discuss family matters fluently in one language but prefer another for academic work. Researchers consequently describe bilingual proficiency as a changing profile of skills rather than two identical versions of monolingual proficiency.','reading') on conflict(code) do update set title=excluded.title,content=excluded.content;
insert into public.assessment_stimuli(code,title,content,stimulus_type) values('KLI-ITP-R5','Community Microgrids','A microgrid is a local energy system that can coordinate electricity generation, storage, and consumption within a limited area. Some microgrids normally connect to a national grid but can operate independently during an outage. This ability, called islanding, can support hospitals, communication equipment, or emergency shelters. Renewable sources such as solar panels are frequently included, yet they create a challenge because their output varies with weather and time of day. Batteries can balance short-term differences between supply and demand, while software decides when to store or release energy. Designing a microgrid also requires social and economic choices. Communities must determine which services receive priority and how costs are shared. A technically sophisticated system may still fail if residents do not understand its rules or trust its operators. Successful projects therefore combine engineering analysis with transparent local decision-making.','reading') on conflict(code) do update set title=excluded.title,content=excluded.content;
